#
# prima-materia — direction of the guided breath, across traditions
#
# One correspondence, held here because it decides a question the collection
# keeps meeting: whether the direction in which a force is led inside the body
# is the direction of the breath itself.
#

pmc:DirectionIsNotThePhaseOfBreathing a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Direction is carried by a graded course, not by the phase of breathing"@en, "Die Richtung trägt eine gestufte Bahn, nicht die Atemphase"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two traditions assign direction inside the body to something other than inhaling and exhaling. One assigns it to two winds driven against each other in a single operation; the other to a place in a graded sequence, with descent in the middle of it and ascent at its end. Neither pairs a direction with a phase of the breath."@en ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:OpposedDirectionsOfPranaAndApana, pmc:XingqiDescent, pmc:YinshuQiXiaXing ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:Daoist ;
    pm:independenceGround "One side carries the claim and the other has been withdrawn from it. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era, earlier than any documented transmission of Indian breath technique into China, and for that witness there is no route of contact to be had. The manual is fifteenth-century and stands downstream of centuries of Buddhist traffic in both directions; it cannot be declared contact-free, and it was listed here as an independent attestation until this ground was required, which is what the requirement is for. Neither witness is an ethnographic gathering, so no recorder's feedback is at issue." ;
    skos:note "The independence is asymmetric and the node says so rather than claiming more than it has. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era on the reading this collection follows, which is earlier than any documented transmission of Indian breath technique into China; for that witness there is no route of contact to be had. The manual is fifteenth-century and stands downstream of centuries of Buddhist traffic in both directions, so its side of the correspondence cannot be declared contact-free with the same confidence. What the two share is therefore better read as one early independent witness and one late witness of uncertain independence, which is weaker than a convergence of two early ones and stronger than a citation chain."@en ;
    skos:note "A third member was added on 2026-09-16 and it changes the asymmetry the note above reports. The Pulling Book from tomb 247 at Zhangjiashan states a direction taken inside the body and the outcome of its taking it: with a staff in one hand, the face to a wall and the breath held, one foot treading the wall, when the qi of the head flows downward the foot will not be numb, the head will not swell and the nose will not be blocked (pmc:YinshuQiXiaXing). The breath is held throughout, so the direction is not carried by a phase of it; what is varied is the posture and the side. The agreement is on the denial and not on what takes the denial's place — the manual has two winds driven against each other, the jade inscription a graded course, and this one a posture — and the node is worded for the denial, so the third member belongs to it."@en ;
    skos:note "What the third member is worth is its date and the way its date is known. The jade inscription is dated on a reading of its script and its content, which is a scholarly judgement and can be revised. This manuscript is dated by the tomb it was sealed in, between 186 and 156 before this era, and nothing was written into it after that. For the independence question that is a different kind of ground: the earlier reasoning had to argue that no route of contact is documented, and this one can say that no route could have reached the object after a fixed year. It does not make the fifteenth-century manual independent, and the asymmetry below still holds; what it does is put two early witnesses on the side that was carrying one."@en ;
    skos:note "A second correspondence sits alongside this one and is deliberately not asserted as a separate node yet: both traditions sanction the wrong handling of the breath with death, the one saying that the breath kills the practitioner who forces it, the other that going against the sequence is to die. Two death sanctions are a thinner finding than they look, since a warning of death is the commonest form a strong prohibition takes anywhere, and the two do not agree on what the fault is: one names haste and force, the other names reversal of order. Recorded here so that the next reader can weigh it rather than rediscover it."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.47 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)", "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125", "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 36 to 37, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" .
